1 CALL

   Runs a subroutine of the same command procedure at a new command
   level, as @ runs a procedure in a file.

   Format

     CALL label [p1 [p2 ... p8]]

2 Parameters

 label

   The label of the subroutine's SUBROUTINE command.

 p1 ... p8

   Up to eight parameters, the subroutine's local symbols P1 to P8, as
   @'s are.

2 Description

   A subroutine is the commands from a label's SUBROUTINE to its
   ENDSUBROUTINE:

       $ label: SUBROUTINE
       $     command ...
       $ ENDSUBROUTINE

   It has parameters, local symbols, and labels of its own.  It ends at
   its ENDSUBROUTINE or at an EXIT, and CALL's status is the
   subroutine's, as @'s is the procedure's.  A procedure run line by
   line passes over its subroutines: they run only when called.  A
   subroutine inside another subroutine, or inside an IF block, can be
   called only from within it.

   A label that isn't found is DCL-W-USCALL.  A label not on a
   SUBROUTINE line runs from there at a new level, and CALL then fails
   with DCL-E-INVCALL.

2 Qualifiers
/OUTPUT=filespec

   Sends the subroutine's output to the file rather than the terminal,
   as @'s /OUTPUT does.

2 Examples

   1.$ CALL GREET "Hello there"
     $ EXIT
     $ GREET: SUBROUTINE
     $     WRITE SYS$OUTPUT P1
     $ ENDSUBROUTINE

     Calls GREET, which writes its first parameter.
